// O3PipeView text timeline format guide (rendered by util/o3-pipeview.py)
// Source trace records are emitted with gem5 debug flag: O3PipeView.
//
// Record columns:
// 1) [timeline] : Fixed-width timeline for one instruction. Each character is
//    one rendered cycle (cycle_time ticks chosen when running o3-pipeview.py).
// 2) -(tick)    : Start tick of this printed timeline window.
// 3) pc.upc     : Macro PC and micro-PC as <pc>.<upc>.
// 4) disasm     : Disassembled instruction text.
// 5) seq_num    : Dynamic instruction sequence number.
//
// Stage letters in [timeline]:
//   f=fetch d=decode n=rename p=dispatch i=issue c=complete r=retire
//   s=store-complete (present only when --store_completions is enabled)
//
// Visual rules:
// - '.' means the instruction retired (committed).
// - '=' means no retire event was recorded (retire tick == 0).
//   Usually this is a squashed wrong-path instruction (flush after branch
//   mispredict/exception), or an in-flight instruction when tracing stopped.
// - If dispatch and issue happen on the same tick, 'p' is omitted.
// - Long-latency instructions wrap to multiple lines for the same seq_num.
//   Continuation lines show only "..." in metadata columns.
// - A disassembly prefixed with "-----" means no commit timestamp
//   (retire tick == 0): typically a squashed instruction; less commonly an
//   in-flight instruction at the end of tracing.
//
// How to read this view quickly:
// - Follow seq_num for program order; compare columns to see pipeline overlap.
// - Larger i->c distance usually means execute or memory latency.
// - Larger c->r distance usually means commit-side backpressure.
// - Many '=' lines usually indicate flush/squash activity.
//
// f = fetch, d = decode, n = rename, p = dispatch, i = issue, c = complete, r = retire

                                     timeline                                             tick          pc.upc     disasm                      seq_num
